faq:
  - q: "Is it better to buy a house now or rent and buy later?"
    a: "The page doesn't decide it for you. It runs both plans on the same savings and the same monthly spending, so the plan that costs less each month puts the difference into bitcoin, and shows what each is worth at the end, after selling costs and tax. The answer turns on the gap between rent and the cost of owning, how fast house prices rise while you wait, and bitcoin's path, which is why the page shows plain what-ifs (bitcoin halves, bitcoin goes nowhere) beside the model's scenarios."
  - q: "Should I put 20% down or 10% down and buy bitcoin with the difference?"
    a: "Set Plan A to 20% down and Plan B to 10% down on the same house, or use the preset. The smaller down payment leaves more cash to put into bitcoin today, at the cost of a bigger loan and more interest every month, which the 20% plan then invests. The page shows the end result under each bitcoin path and the risks of carrying the larger loan."
  - q: "How does the page keep the comparison fair?"
    a: "Both households start with the same savings, the larger of the two plans' up-front needs, and every month both spend what the dearer plan's housing costs that month. Whatever a plan doesn't spend on housing goes into bitcoin. If a plan has to find money it doesn't have, for a down payment later or a month its costs are higher, it sells bitcoin, and anything beyond that comes from income and is counted against the plan."
  - q: "Where do the default numbers come from?"
    a: "The house is $415,000, the 2025 median price of a new US home; rent is market rent for that house from Zillow's price-to-rent ratio (15.8); closing, property tax, insurance, maintenance and selling costs are the series' sourced defaults; the mortgage rate is 7.0%, Freddie Mac's 30-year average in late September 2026 (7.03%). Change any of them."
