<section class="hero">
    <h1>Compare <em>Housing Plans</em></h1>
    <div class="hero-subtitle">Two ways to house yourself, side by side: buy now or rent and buy later, 20% down or 10% with the difference in bitcoin, this house or a cheaper one.</div>
    <p class="subtitle">Both plans start with the same savings and spend the same every month; whatever a plan doesn&rsquo;t spend on housing goes into bitcoin. The page shows what each is worth at the end, under stated assumptions, and doesn&rsquo;t pick one.</p>
</section>

<main class="content-area">

{% include 'components/real-estate-series.njk' %}

{% include 'components/tool-framing.njk' %}

<section class="ch-calc" id="calculator" aria-labelledby="chCalcTitle">
    <h2 class="ch-h2" id="chCalcTitle">Your two plans</h2>
    <div class="ch-presets" role="group" aria-label="Start from a common comparison">
        <span class="ch-presets-k">Start from</span>
        <button type="button" class="ch-chip" data-preset="later">Buy now vs. buy in 3 years</button>
        <button type="button" class="ch-chip" data-preset="down">20% down vs. 10% down</button>
        <button type="button" class="ch-chip" data-preset="cheaper">This house vs. a cheaper one</button>
        <button type="button" class="ch-chip" data-preset="rent">Buy vs. rent</button>
    </div>

    <div class="ch-plans">
        {% for x in [{ p: 'a', name: 'Plan A' }, { p: 'b', name: 'Plan B' }] %}
        <div class="ch-plan {{ x.p }}" id="chPlan{{ x.p | upper }}">
            <h3>{{ x.name }}</h3>
            <div class="ch-plan-grid">
                <label class="ch-field full"><span class="k">The plan</span>
                    <select id="ch_{{ x.p }}_kind" aria-label="{{ x.name }}: the plan"><option value="now">Buy today</option><option value="later">Rent, then buy</option><option value="rent">Rent the whole time</option></select></label>
                <label class="ch-field"><span class="k">Home price today<span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">The house this plan buys, or rents, at today&rsquo;s price. A plan that buys later pays that year&rsquo;s price, grown at the home-appreciation rate. Rent is market rent for this house.</span></span></span>
                    <span class="ch-in ch-in-usd"><input id="ch_{{ x.p }}_price" type="text" inputmode="numeric" aria-label="{{ x.name }}: home price today"></span></label>
                <label class="ch-field" data-own><span class="k">Down payment</span>
                    <span class="ch-in ch-in-pct"><input id="ch_{{ x.p }}_down" type="text" inputmode="decimal" aria-label="{{ x.name }}: down payment, percent"></span></label>
                <label class="ch-field" data-own><span class="k">Mortgage rate<span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">A 30-year fixed rate. For a plan that buys later, the rate it expects to get then; the page doesn&rsquo;t forecast rates.</span></span></span>
                    <span class="ch-in ch-in-pct"><input id="ch_{{ x.p }}_rate" type="text" inputmode="decimal" aria-label="{{ x.name }}: mortgage rate"></span></label>
                <label class="ch-field" data-later><span class="k">Buy in year</span>
                    <span class="ch-in"><input id="ch_{{ x.p }}_year" type="text" inputmode="numeric" aria-label="{{ x.name }}: buy in year"></span></label>
            </div>
        </div>
        {% endfor %}
    </div>

    <details class="ch-assume" id="chAssume">
        <summary><span>Shared assumptions: years, homes, costs, tax</span> <span class="ch-assume-hint" id="chAssumeHint"></span></summary>
        <div class="ch-assume-body">
            <label class="ch-field"><span class="k">Years to compare</span><span class="ch-in"><input id="chHorizon" type="text" inputmode="numeric" value="10" aria-label="Years to compare"></span></label>
            <label class="ch-field"><span class="k">Home appreciation<span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">Nominal, a year, for both plans&rsquo; houses. Default: the site&rsquo;s setting (Since 2000, about 4.7%), the same as the other real-estate pages. Rent grows at the same rate.</span></span></span><span class="ch-in ch-in-pct"><input id="chAppr" type="text" inputmode="decimal" aria-label="Home appreciation, percent a year"></span></label>
            <label class="ch-field"><span class="k">Closing costs (buying)</span><span class="ch-in ch-in-pct"><input id="chClosing" type="text" inputmode="decimal" value="1.04" aria-label="Closing costs, percent of price"></span></label>
            <label class="ch-field"><span class="k">Property tax</span><span class="ch-in ch-in-pct"><input id="chPropTax" type="text" inputmode="decimal" value="0.9" aria-label="Property tax, percent of value a year"></span></label>
            <label class="ch-field"><span class="k">Maintenance</span><span class="ch-in ch-in-pct"><input id="chMaint" type="text" inputmode="decimal" value="1" aria-label="Maintenance, percent of value a year"></span></label>
            <label class="ch-field"><span class="k">Selling costs</span><span class="ch-in ch-in-pct"><input id="chSell" type="text" inputmode="decimal" value="6.6" aria-label="Selling costs, percent of price"></span></label>
            <label class="ch-field"><span class="k">Bitcoin trading cost</span><span class="ch-in ch-in-pct"><input id="chTx" type="text" inputmode="decimal" value="0.5" aria-label="Bitcoin trading cost, percent"></span></label>
            <label class="ch-field"><span class="k">Federal bracket</span>
                <select id="chBracket" aria-label="Federal tax bracket"><option value="12">12%</option><option value="22">22%</option><option value="24" selected>24%</option><option value="32">32%</option><option value="35">35%</option><option value="37">37%</option></select></label>
            <label class="ch-field"><span class="k">State</span><select id="chState" aria-label="State for tax"></select></label>
            <label class="ch-field"><span class="k">Tax on gains</span>
                <select id="chTax" aria-label="Tax on gains"><option value="us" selected>US rules</option><option value="none">No capital-gains tax</option></select></label>
            <label class="ch-field ch-check"><input id="chDed" type="checkbox"> <span>I itemize and deduct mortgage interest</span></label>
            <div class="ch-field"><span class="k">Dollars shown</span>
                <div class="re-seg" role="group" aria-label="Dollars shown"><button type="button" class="re-seg-btn active" data-display="real" aria-pressed="true">Today&rsquo;s dollars</button><button type="button" class="re-seg-btn" data-display="nominal" aria-pressed="false">Future dollars</button></div>
                <span class="ch-small" id="chDeflLine"></span></div>
        </div>
    </details>

    <div class="ch-scen" role="group" aria-label="Bitcoin scenario">
        <span class="ch-scen-k">Bitcoin scenario<span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">The series&rsquo; four paths from the <a href="/the-power-law">Power Law</a>, each moving bitcoin from where it sits today against the trend to a target at the end: <strong>Drifts to the floor</strong>, the model&rsquo;s lower line (0.42&times; trend); <strong>Today&rsquo;s gap persists</strong> (the default); <strong>Reverts to trend</strong>; <strong>Peaks at 2.5&times; trend</strong>, a stress test. The table under the cards adds two plain what-ifs.</span></span></span>
        <button type="button" class="ch-chip" data-scenario="floor">Drifts to the floor</button>
        <button type="button" class="ch-chip on" data-scenario="stay">Today&rsquo;s gap persists</button>
        <button type="button" class="ch-chip" data-scenario="trend">Reverts to trend</button>
        <button type="button" class="ch-chip" data-scenario="upper">Peaks at 2.5&times; trend</button>
    </div>

    <div class="ch-hurdle" id="chHead" aria-live="polite">
        <div class="ch-hurdle-k">The difference</div>
        <div class="ch-hurdle-v" id="chHeadV">&hellip;</div>
        <p class="ch-hurdle-s" id="chHeadS">The calculator needs JavaScript.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="ch-cashout" id="chCashOut"></p>
    <div class="ch-cards" id="chCards"></div>

    <div class="ch-table-wrap"><table class="ch-table" id="chScenTable" aria-label="The difference under each bitcoin path"></table></div>

    <div class="re-chart-block" id="chChartBlock">
        <div class="re-chart-head">
            <div class="re-chart-title">The two plans over time <span class="re-chart-frame" id="chChartFrame"></span></div>
            <div class="re-seg" role="group" aria-label="What the lines value"><button type="button" class="re-seg-btn active" data-basis="ifsold" aria-pressed="true">If sold, after tax</button><button type="button" class="re-seg-btn" data-basis="held" aria-pressed="false">Held</button></div>
        </div>
        <div class="re-chart-wrap"><canvas id="chChart"></canvas></div>
        <p class="re-chart-note">Each line is a plan&rsquo;s wealth each year: its house (if it owns one) less the loan, plus its bitcoin, less anything it had to find from income. <em>If sold, after tax</em> sells both that year; <em>Held</em> is before any sale. The heaviest, light line is the <strong>difference</strong>, Plan B minus Plan A.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="chLedger"></div>
</section>

<section class="ch-prose" id="risks" aria-labelledby="chRiskTitle">
    <h2 class="ch-h2" id="chRiskTitle">What the numbers leave out</h2>
    <div class="ch-risks">
        <div class="ch-risk">
            <h3>Owning</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>One asset, borrowed against.</strong> A mortgage is leverage on a single house in a single town. The page grows every house at the same rate; yours won&rsquo;t.</li>
                <li><strong>Hard to leave.</strong> Selling costs about 6.6% and takes months. A smaller down payment means a bigger loan, more interest, and less room if prices fall.</li>
                <li><strong>Costs the page averages.</strong> A new roof is a lump, not 1% a year.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="ch-risk">
            <h3>Renting and holding bitcoin</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Rent can outrun the model.</strong> The page grows rent at the home-appreciation rate; local rents jump, and leases end.</li>
                <li><strong>Buying later means selling bitcoin then.</strong> A plan that buys in year three sells bitcoin for the down payment at that year&rsquo;s price, which could be in a deep fall. If the bitcoin doesn&rsquo;t cover it, the rest comes from income and the card says so.</li>
                <li><strong>Prices can run away.</strong> Waiting costs more if house prices rise faster than the setting.</li>
                <li><strong>Bitcoin can fall a long way:</strong> 73% or more four times between month-end closes since 2011. The table above shows the plans if it halves, or goes nowhere.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="ch-prose" id="method" aria-labelledby="chMethodTitle">
    <h2 class="ch-h2" id="chMethodTitle">How the page computes</h2>
    <ul class="ch-method">
        <li><strong>The same savings, the same spending.</strong> Both plans start with the larger of their two up-front needs (a down payment plus closing costs for a plan that buys today). Each month both spend what the dearer plan&rsquo;s housing costs that month; the cheaper plan puts the difference into bitcoin. That is the rule the other real-estate pages use, stated for two plans.</li>
        <li><strong>Owning</strong> costs the 30-year loan&rsquo;s payment, property tax, insurance and maintenance, the last three following the home&rsquo;s value; <strong>renting</strong> costs market rent for the same house, growing at the home-appreciation rate. A plan that buys later rents until the start of its purchase year, then buys at that year&rsquo;s price, selling bitcoin for the down payment and closing costs.</li>
        <li><strong>At the end</strong> each plan&rsquo;s house is sold, less selling costs, the loan and any tax above the home-sale exclusion (and only after two years of owning), and its bitcoin is sold, less the trading cost and the tax on its gain. Money a plan had to find from income is subtracted.</li>
        <li><strong>The engine</strong> is the one behind <a href="/bitcoin-vs-real-estate">Bitcoin vs. Real Estate</a>; set Plan A to buy and Plan B to rent the same house and the page&rsquo;s check reproduces that page&rsquo;s figures to the cent.</li>
        <li><strong>Not modelled:</strong> private mortgage insurance on a down payment under 20%, typically a fraction of a percent of the loan a year until enough is paid off, which leaving out flatters the smaller down payment; moving costs; renters&rsquo; insurance; refinancing; and rates or prices that differ from the settings.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

</main>
